> In particular, DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.* and
> DECW$DEVICE*.COM.  For an ELSA GLoria Synergy card, look for
> "GZ".  Searching the archives of this group may find many
> things.
> 
>    In my DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.COM, for example, one might
> see:

The template contains:

$ ! First, replace the definition of node_list with your own list of cluster
$ ! node names separated by "/".  Next, at the end of this file, add a section
$ ! for each node consisting of: a label of the form "DO_NODENAME:" (where
$ ! NODENAME is the name of the particular node), followed by DCL commands
$ ! that specify the setup options for the particular node, followed by the
$ ! DCL command "$ exit".
$ !
$ node_list = ""
$ !
$ ! An example of a real list might be:
$ !
$ ! node_list = "BW75/DUTCH/DUTCH2/UK100/LK201/TCPIP"
$ !
$ if node_list .eqs. "" then goto DO_DEFAULT
$ node_number = 0
$node_loop:
$ node = f$element(node_number, "/", node_list)
$ if node .eqs. "/" then goto DO_DEFAULT
$ if node .eqs. f$getsyi("NODENAME") then goto DO_'node
$ node_number =  node_number + 1
$ goto node_loop

The logic of this is obvious, and is described above.  So far, so good.
But what the DO_NODENAME code blocks in the code do is set up various 
options.  OK, one might want the corresponding stuff to be done for 
particular nodes, but of course the real node names should be in the 
list.  It thus seems far-fetched that this could be an "example of a real 
list".  Presumably, one would replace the mnemonics both in the list and 
in the labels with the node names and put code appropriate for that node 
there.  I've put your suggestions there for a node I'm testing and we'll 
see what happens.

>    Of course, with an EV6 CPU (as in an XP1000), the ATI Radeon
> card ("GH") can be used, which is, I believe, classier than
> the ELSA card.

Right.  I haven't got that far yet.  I plan to have the new monitor on 
an XP1000, but for now am testing it on the only node I have in the 
cluster with a VGA connection, which is an ALPHAserver 1200.  I wanted 
to makes sure the monitor works OK first before making sure the new 
machine is configured correctly.

Is the resolution dependent only on the graphics card?  In other words, 
can any graphics monitor support any resolution (up to 1024x1280, at 
least)?  Or could it be that some monitors will support only 768x1024 
even if the graphics card supports more?

It seems to me one would want to put stuff in
DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.COM based on the name of the graphics device,
not on the node name.  Several nodes might have the same device type,
and a given node might get its graphics card replaced with another type.



