Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:

> [...]
> Is the resolution dependent only on the graphics card?

   Yes and no.  The graphics card and its driver determine the
possible resolutions and refresh rates.  The capabilities of
the display determine which of those combinations it can
display.  (Or, on some older displays, which combinations
won't damage the display.)

>   In other words,
> can any graphics monitor support any resolution (up to 1024x1280, at
> least)?  Or could it be that some monitors will support only 768x1024
> even if the graphics card supports more?

   I had an old (small, 15- or 17-inch) Sony CRT which
couldn't do 1280x1024.  (I may still have it, somewhere.)
Many old displays were fixed at 1024x768, as I recall.  This
would have been typical in the days of the VAXstation 3100
model 30/38/40/48.  (I believe that the optional SPX graphics
card provided 1280x1024, but all my VAXstations 3100 had the
base graphics.)  Not many of those displays still work, I
suspect.

   It's hard to find a modern display which couldn't do
1280x1024, but a typical VMS workstation, or VMS for
workstations, isn't particularly modern.  Which is why, so far
as I know, VMS can't talk to the display and learn what kind
it is.  My Mac, on the other hand knows that it's connected to
a Sony/Sun GDM-5510.  (My XP1000 is also connected to one of
those, but it has no clue as to what it is.)

> It seems to me one would want to put stuff in
> DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.COM based on the name of the graphics device,
> not on the node name.  [...]

   In that example piece I provided earlier, I seem to have
left out a significant part.  (I forgot that it was my work,
not part of the template.)

[...]
$ dwd0 = -
   f$edit( f$extract( 0, 2, f$element( 0, ",", DECW$DEVICE)),
"UPCASE")
[...]

That's why a test on "dwd0" makes sense:

[...]
$!
$! GZ (ELSA GLoria Synergy in PWS) currently uses high-res display.
$!
$ if (dwd0 .eqs. "GZ")
$ then
[...]

So, if you care only about the card, and not the actual
display device, it's easy enough to do.


