In comp.os.vms Steven Schweda <sms.antinode@gmail.com> wrote:
(snip)

>   Yes and no.  The graphics card and its driver determine the
> possible resolutions and refresh rates.  The capabilities of
> the display determine which of those combinations it can
> display.  (Or, on some older displays, which combinations
> won't damage the display.)

For LCD monitors, there are discrete pixels on the screen, and
the graphics card resolution should match that.  If not, it interpolates
such that the result looks blurry.  For CRT monitors, the resolution
limit is mostly the bandwidth of the video amplifier, though the shadow
mask also has some effect.  The horizontal and vertical resolution
also have to be compatible with the monitor, though more for CRTs
where they drive the deflection coils.
 
>>   In other words,
>> can any graphics monitor support any resolution (up to 1024x1280, at
>> least)?  Or could it be that some monitors will support only 768x1024
>> even if the graphics card supports more?

Yes.

-- glen


