Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:

> With reasonably new (colour, mid-1990s or later) monitors, is there any
> need to worry about a wrong combination damaging the display?

   For older CRT displays, especially fixed-scan-rate
displays, the horizontal sweep circuits can be fried if they
are driven at too high a rate.  In the old (UNIX workstation)
days, this was seldom a problem, but when Windows PC systems
started to appear with multi-scan-rate displays, all kinds of
things became possible, not all of them good.  Modern
displays, which are expected to work at multiple scan rates,
are generally smart enough not to try to run at rates which
would damage them, so you get a black screen (and perhaps a
flashing LED, or some other indication) rather than a smoke
signal.  In any case, choosing 1024x768 (60Hz?) as a default
resolution/sweep rate makes it more likely that you'll see
something (other than smoke) no matter what you connect to the
system.  If you want more, then you can get it, but there's no
convenient interactive pop-up/menu method on VMS.  (Or on most
UNIX systems, either?  GNU/Linux, with its PC-hardware
orientation probably does.  Mac and Windows, of course, have
easy-change methods.)

> >    I had an old (small, 15- or 17-inch) Sony CRT which
> > couldn't do 1280x1024.  (I may still have it, somewhere.)
> > Many old displays were fixed at 1024x768, as I recall.  This
> > would have been typical in the days of the VAXstation 3100
> > model 30/38/40/48.
>
> OK.  Probably a b/w monitor?

    Color or monochrome.  (The small Sony was color.  DEC did
sell 19-inch (and that's 19 real inches) monochrome displays
with old VAXstations, if gray was good enough for you.  Color,
too.


