Vlems <hvlems@freenet.de> writes: 

> My rule of thumb is that if a device (with a DEC logo) is fitted with
> VHDCI then it's wide and  runs at 40 MB/s.
> Something similar for 50 pin devices, narrow and runs at 5 or 10 MB/s.

Right.  Ans if it is fitted with HD68, it is wide and runs at 40 MB/s as 
well.

On my collection of various and sundry ALPHA hardware (basically 1990s),
I seem to have just a couple of SCSI cards, the ISP1040 (at the console,
which VMS sees as the ISP1020), which is apparently ultra wide (40
MB/s), and the NCR810, which is apparently fast narrow (10 MB/s).  The
former have HD68, the latter HD50 connectors.  First-generation ALPHAs
and VAXstations have the NCR53C94, which I believe is slow narrow (5
MB/s) and has either an HD50 or CENTRONICS50 connectors.  I haven't run
across any fast wide controllers (20 MB/s).  (Narrow ultra* connectors
apparently exist, apparently for the slower ultra* speeds, but I suspect
they are rare.) 

I have some newer cards with VHDCI (usually 2, side by side or one above
the other) connectors, but either my machines don't see them (perhaps
they aren't meant for this hardware) or they are not SCSI cards but
rather RAID controllers.  Something I haven't checked yet is a 
second-generation EV4 ALPHA (ALPHAserver 400 4/233) with a VHDCI card 
built in (probably not factory installed).



