Thanks for that.

From memory, a VRT19-HA is 3BNC with sync-on-green at a limited number
of fixed frequencies (ie not multisync).  Great with a compatible
VAXstation or earlier Alpha graphics. Long in the tooth now.

Bill Pedersen's link confirms the fixed-frequency sync-on-green and
offers some options for (software or hardware) hackery for using such
a monitor with a commodity graphics card and a commodity OS (Linux,
some old Windows). Equivalent hackery may or may not be practical with
VMS and your XP1000's graphics card...

As Steven Schweda points out, those of us who are not telepathetic
still don't know what graphics is in your XP1000. A suitable QuickSpec
[1] says multiple options were available but unless it's been
retrofitted with a ZLXp-whatever (which seems like a backward step) or
you want to make/buy something to go from VGA to sync-on-green or you
want to look into the hackery in the link which Bill P provided, it
seems likely you are out of luck (but likely .ne. certain). A standard
VGA to 3BNC cable may confirm this for a small cost, although to be
100% certain you'd need to check there were no DIP switches or other
(software?) options to change the graphics card config (refresh rate
and resolution).

The transition between DEC-specific and high-end "commodity" graphics
was around the same time as the transition from TURBOchannel (etc) to
PCI. Can't immediately relate that to EV4/5, maybe you can. But the
QuickSpec says that the cards in common use in an XP1000 are from the
VGA era not the sync-on-green era.

As Richard G says, a KVM switch is a device for switching one keyboard/
video/mouse setup between multiple computers (typically, but not
always, servers in the computer room). In the modern world where
decent servers such as Proliant have "integrated lights out
management" and where VMware and/or Windows are often the only options
that count, KVM switches are less relevant than they used to be, but
real KVM switches are largely hardware-independent and OS-independent
and can still be used in circumstances where software-only "solutions"
such as VNC, RDP, etc are not an option. Not all KVM switches are
equal; I recently needed to expand my home setup from a Belkin
Omnicube 4port and acquired a dirt cheap CPQ one (8 ports, cascadable
to support 64 or so computers) but sadly the CPQ can't cope with
modern desktop resolutions and refresh rates (why would it, it's
intended for *server* management, before the days of "Integrated
Lights Out"). Anyway I now have a Belkin 8port OmniView Pro - it was
cheap (including cables) on eBay, and does the job so far with every
computer and every OS and every sensible monitor resolution I have
thrown at it.

Best of luck anyway.


[1] http://h18000.www1.hp.com/products/quickspecs/10030_na/10030_na.pdf


