In comp.os.vms H Vlems <hvlems@freenet.de> wrote:
> On Dec 19, 9:04�pm, hel...@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de (Phillip Helbig---
> undress to reply) wrote:
(snip)

>> If the BA356 is the limiting speed, do I need a blue box?
 
> What would determine the speed is either the electronics or 
> the design of the wiring inside the BA35.
> Even if it is a passive box (no active components) then things like
> the characteristic impedence set the bus transfer rates.

Well, more attenuation than impedance.  

> Characteristic impedance is a property common to all data carriers.
> Thinwire ethernet has it, 50 ohms IIRC.

> If you plug a device with the correct characteristic impedance in a
> bus that suits it then the total impedance of that bus won't change.

> Amalog phones work like that. The char. imp. was 600 ohms (in NL) and
> as soon as customers plugged in awkward gear the PTT started to
> complain.

For analog phones, 600 ohms is not the cable impedance, but the
source impedance.  For long runs, the series resistance of the
wire becomes most important, and so a higher source impedance
is better.  As the cable gets longer, the capacitance in
parallel and resistance in series form a low pass filter,
reducing the high frequencies.  To compensate for that,
series inductors are placed at specified distances along
the cable.  That raises the response below 4kHz, but it drops
like a rock after that.  Fine for voice, not so good for DSL.

> Mismatches resulted in poor audio quality. Also in out of phase
> current and voltages which was what the phone carrier noticed.
> The same probably applies to the BA35, though at faster rates of
> course.

> Which is a very long way of saying, yes Phillip, I'm afraid you need a
> blue box. marktplaats.nl had two of them for sale recently.
> Otherwise I might know a place.

The other problem with poor boxes is non-uniform impedance, 
especially discontinuities which cause reflections.

-- glen


