I'm in the process of testing various used hardware.  I probably don't
have two machines configured the same, much less more than two.  Also,
some things don't work on some machines.  So, I'm trying to maximise the
number of working machines and also trying to configure as many machines
as possible as similarly as possible, to enable quick swapping of the
entire machine if something stops working.  (I can then take my time and
see what was wrong.  For the same reason, I am moving to SBB disks
exclusively.)  Some things I probably won't go to the trouble to swap,
at least for now, since it is difficult.  Other stuff, such as PCI
cards, can be swapped quite easily.  They tend to work in a wide variety
of machines.  (This is also apparent from the fact that I seem to have
just 3 different types of SCSI card in much more than 3 types of
machine, ditto for ethernet cards and graphic cards.)  If they don't
work, then usually there is no harm done. 

Memory is somewhere in-between.  It can be swapped without much effort, 
but it is fragile, so I don't want to do it too much.  Also, if it 
doesn't work, it's not clear if it is because there is no way it can 
work or due to a loose connection etc.  On the other hand, if I could 
swap memory freely this would probably offer me the most improvement.

Restricting ourselves to just ALPHA machines: if the wrong memory is in 
the machine, will it just not work or can it be damaged?

Then there is the question of which banks must contain identical cards.

Is there any source of information detailing which memory can be used in 
which machine?  (Official documentation exists, of course, but a) would 
take a lot of effort to track down and b) would probably apply to only 
DEC memory.)

At the moment, I'm interested in the possibility of swapping between the 
following machines:

   o  ALPHAstation 255/233

   o  ALPHAstation 500/500

   o  PWS (various models)

   o  Digital Server 5305 a.k.a. ALPHAserver 1200

   o  XP1000 (various models)



