In article <ie13q9$fk3$2@online.de>, helbig@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de (Phillip Helbig---undress to reply) writes:
>A few weeks ago, I was testing a PWS and found that the familiar console
>was forgotten after a power down, presumably because the battery was
>bad.  I am now testing another one which also presumably has a bad 
>battery, since it asks me for the date and time when booting.  However, 
>on this machine, the only console I have ever seen is the familiar one.

You mean AlphaBIOS (or ARC on earlier AlphaStations) versus SRM?

>Were there separate models, some with just the familiar VMS console and 
>some with several (and a non-VMS one as the default)?

There were "half-flashed" and "full-flashed" AlphaStations.
"Half-flashed" could only contain one console (ARC or SRM).
So the couldn't switch to the wrong one in case of a battery loss.

btw: I never had a "half-flashed" Alpha so far (and I had umpteen of them)

But on the PWS I never heard of half-flashed ones so far.
Maybe they existed, I don't know. But PWS were later than AlphaStations
and prices fell (so I see no real reason for a half flashed variant).

The PWS came in 2 flavours however. The A and the AU
While the A was marketed for WNT (and AlphaBIOS), the hardware wasn't
really different to an AU. Only the graphic and the disk controllers were
limited to supported (by OSF1/TRU64 or VMS) ones on the AU.
"U" for Universal (not UNIX) as it ran all the operating systems then.
In fact, all my original AU ones called themselves A at POST time.

To be more correct: the PWS had 2 different models: MIATA GL and MIATA MX
The earlier one had an INTEL SIO and you couldn't boot VMS from the IDE CD.
But I don't think, the flash was different.

>Also, if the console is set to "serial", when booting it switches to the 
>graphics console if there is one connected.  Other ALPHA systems I have 
>will boot with the display on the serial console even if a monitor is 
>connected.  Is there any way to avoid this, short of disconnecting the 
>cable to the monitor?  (Disconnecting just the keyboard doesn't do it.)

Sorry, I don't know/remember now.
I think, I had output on graphic and serial but input only on one,
(but I may be wrong and it was after the PWS systems)...

