In comp.sys.dec Phillip Helbig---undress to reply <helbig@astro.multiclothesvax.de> wrote:
>
> Restricting ourselves to just ALPHA machines: if the wrong memory is in 
> the machine, will it just not work or can it be damaged?

I have never heard of incompatible memory breaking something.
 
> At the moment, I'm interested in the possibility of swapping between the 
> following machines:
> 
>   o  ALPHAstation 255/233

All the Alphastation 200/400 (Avanti) class machines use 72pin SIMMs with
real parity. Some want sets of two modules, others need sets of four SIMMs.
 
>   o  ALPHAstation 500/500

That's special RAM. I think it was some type of EDO DIMM,
but I might be wrong here. Unless you feel like experimenting,
I wouldn't get any 3-party RAM.
 
>   o  PWS (various models)

This one uses PC100 ECC SD-RAM. The RAM is actually used at a slower
speed than PC100, but most modules will work. You need regular
not-registered modules.
 
>   o  Digital Server 5305 a.k.a. ALPHAserver 1200

I never had one of those.
 
>   o  XP1000 (various models)

uses PC100 ECC SD-RAM. this time registered! see PWS.
The XP1000 uses sets of four modules. 2GB memory is the maximum
here. There are larger modules available, but the system will
simply not recognize any memory modules larger than 256MB.

Dennis

