> hopefully crash or at least report some ECC errors if memory
> is broken. Either way, it is very time consuming.

I'm not familiar with x86 mem testers,
but tests which require the OS to be loaded
come much too late in the boot sequence, IMHO.
It is my (maybe limited) understanding
that BIST/POST procedures should rigorously
test the hardware and tell me it's 99+x% OK
to run the supported OS on.

On my PWS roughly half of the RAM modules I tested
were accepted at the POST stage but crashed the OS
miserably. Relatively few were rejected right away.
With such a low recognition rate one could just skip
the RAM test in the boot procedure, it's almost
worthless.

I had an even worse experience with a DECstation 5260.
I was quite happy to be able to fully load it with RAM,
console says it's OK, OS booted, but some memory intensive
tasks simply froze the machine (iirc heavy "nedit" sessions).
It took me about half a day of RAM swapping until my fingers
bled to get a stable machine.

I had different experience with PS/2 based machines (non-DEC though).
Their 72-pin RAM may come in non/parity and ECC flavour.
All boxes I've tried so far simply rejected to boot (some
even rejected to power up) if some "wrong" module was inserted.



