Dennis Grevenstein schrieb:
> Michael Kraemer <M.Kraemer@gsi.de> wrote:
> 

> 
> that's a common problem for all the older systems.
> I have similar stories for Suns and SGIs.

I have no problems to accept that from a pile
of 20 year old RAM modules some are not good
anymore. But couldn't the f***ing hardware
test tell me which one beforehand?
Especially if one thinks of how expensive
both, the machine and its proprietary RAM
were back in their time?

> parity or ECC is a nice thing, but often not enough
> when something is really broken.
>  
> 
>>I had different experience with PS/2 based machines (non-DEC though).
>>Their 72-pin RAM may come in non/parity and ECC flavour.
>>All boxes I've tried so far simply rejected to boot (some
>>even rejected to power up) if some "wrong" module was inserted.
> 
>  
> do you mean that the box is detecting parity vs non-parity?
> That is something very different than really testing the
> memory.

one of my favourite PS/2 RAM testers is a Motorola
PowerStack (the black one). It's RAM slots are
easily accessible and it takes non parity as well
as parity (but not ECC), from 4MB (iirc) to 64MB.
If it doesn't like the RAM at all, it doesn't boot/powerup.
If it can deal with it, it'll properly test it.
None of the "OK" RAM failed when inserted in another box.
Similarly, my Indigo2, RS6000's and several HP 9000's told me right
away when they didn't like the RAM. None of these crashed their OS
due to badly tested RAM.
Maybe PS/2 RAM tests are more stringent?



