On 8/12/2010 11:44 AM, Johnny Billquist wrote:
> On 2010-02-19 23:52, Tom Lake wrote:
>> When I'm in the BASIC-Plus-2 environment (under RSTS/E), I try to do and
>> DCL commands such as DIR but I never return to the BASIC2 prompt after
>> the command is completed. Is this a bug
>> or is my copy corrupted?
>
> Wow. Long time since this was asked, but anyway...
> It might just be the way things are. BASIC+2 is a layered product. In
> RSTS/E a user can only run one program at a time, and a program don't
> know what it was chanined to from, so when you invoke a DCL command such
> as DIR, a program is run, and it don't know that it should restart the
> program BASIC+2 afterwards in that case.
>
> This is a difference between a layered product, like BASIC+2, and an
> RTS, like BASIC+, under RSTS/E...
>
> Johnny
>

When you install BASIC-PLUS-2, a non-default installation dialog 
(answering NO to the "Do you want the default installation?" question) 
will let you select a build of the BP2 run time system.

If this BASIC-PLUS-2 runtime system is subsequently made the active 
keyboard monitor, then DCL commands (lines you enter with a dollar sign 
as the first character) will pass to DCL to be executed and then return 
to the BASIC2 prompt.

$ show runtime

Run-Time Systems:
  Name   Typ   Dev    Size    Users  Comments
...RSX  TSK          0(66)K    6    Monitor, KBM
DCL     COM   DU0:   24(8)K    0    Temp, Addr:103, DF KBM
RT11    SAV   DU0:   4(28)K    0    Temp, Addr:149, KBM, CSZ, EMT:255
BASIC   BAC   DU0:  16(16)K    0    Non-Res, KBM, CSZ
TECO    TEC   DU0:  10(20)K    0    Non-Res, KBM
BP2           DU0:    1(1)K    0    Non-Res, KBM
$ switch bp2		! the $ is the DCL prompt

PDP-11 BASIC-PLUS-2 V2.7-00

BASIC2

$ dir *.txt		! must type the $ character

  Name .Typ    Size    Prot     Name .Typ    Size    Prot    SY:[11,42]
RSTSE .TXT       7   < 40>

Total of 7 blocks in 1 file in SY:[11,42]


PDP-11 BASIC-PLUS-2 V2.7-00

BASIC2

$			! enter $ on a line by itself to return to DCL
$ 			! this is the DCL prompt again

I never used the interactive mode of BASIC-PLUS-2 much - preferring 
instead to use iterations of the text editor, compile and link commands.


Tony


